<!-- PRACTICAL 6                                -->
<!-- ========================================== -->
<h2>Practical 6: Rendering External JSON Data using Fetch API, Search & Filter</h2>
<p><strong>Problem Definition:</strong> Display campus event lists from an external JSON file (<code>events.json</code> with 15+ records) using the Fetch API, implementing dynamic rendering, search, category filter, sorting, and pagination.</p>

<h3>1. Key Questions & Analysis:</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>How is JSON fetched and parsed?</strong> Using <code>fetch('events.json').then(r =&gt; r.json())</code> with async/await and error-handling try/catch blocks.</li>
  <li><strong>Which array methods are used?</strong> <code>Array.filter()</code> for name/venue search and category filtering; <code>Array.sort()</code> for chronological date ordering; <code>Array.slice(start, start + perPage)</code> for pagination.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Key JavaScript Snippet (dynamic.html):</h3>
<div class="code-box">async function loadEvents() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch('events.json');
    allEvents = await res.json();
    renderEvents();
  } catch (err) {
    document.getElementById('eventStatus').textContent = 'Error loading events.json';
  }
}

function getFilteredEvents() {
  const q = searchInput.value.toLowerCase();
  const cat = categoryFilter.value;
  return allEvents.filter(e =&gt; 
    (e.name.toLowerCase().includes(q) || e.venue.toLowerCase().includes(q)) &&
    (cat === '' || e.category === cat)
  );
}</div>

