<!-- PRACTICAL 7                                -->
<!-- ========================================== -->
<h2>Practical 7: PHP Form Processing with Server-Side Validation & CSV/JSON Storage</h2>
<p><strong>Problem Definition:</strong> Process submitted registration/feedback data using PHP POST method, validate and sanitize inputs on the server side, and store records in CSV and JSON formats.</p>

<h3>1. Key Questions & Analysis:</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Why is server-side validation mandatory?</strong> Client-side validation can be disabled or bypassed; server-side sanitization (<code>filter_input</code>, <code>htmlspecialchars</code>) ensures malicious data never harms the application.</li>
  <li><strong>How is safe file writing handled?</strong> Using <code>fopen($file, 'a')</code>, <code>fputcsv()</code> with automatic headers, and <code>file_put_contents($jsonFile, json_encode($data, JSON_PRETTY_PRINT))</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Key PHP Snippet (feedback.php):</h3>
<div class="code-box">$name = trim(filter_input(INPUT_POST, "name", FILTER_SANITIZE_SPECIAL_CHARS));
$email = trim(filter_input(INPUT_POST, "email", FILTER_SANITIZE_EMAIL));
$rating = filter_input(INPUT_POST, "rating", FILTER_VALIDATE_INT);
$comments = trim(filter_input(INPUT_POST, "comments", FILTER_SANITIZE_SPECIAL_CHARS));

if ($name && filter_var($email, FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL)) {
  // 1. Store in CSV
  $handle = fopen("feedback.csv", "a");
  fputcsv($handle, [date("Y-m-d H:i:s"), $name, $email, $rating, $comments]);
  fclose($handle);

  // 2. Store in JSON
  $records = file_exists("feedback.json") ? json_decode(file_get_contents("feedback.json"), true) : [];
  $records[] = ["timestamp" =&gt; date("Y-m-d H:i:s"), "name" =&gt; $name, "rating" =&gt; $rating, "comments" =&gt; $comments];
  file_put_contents("feedback.json", json_encode($records, JSON_PRETTY_PRINT));
}</div>

