<!-- PRACTICAL 9                                -->
<!-- ========================================== -->
<h2>Practical 9: Secure User Registration with Password Hashing & Duplicate Check</h2>
<p><strong>Problem Definition:</strong> Implement secure user registration inserting user details into MySQL. Validate inputs on frontend and backend, check for duplicate emails, and hash passwords using <code>password_hash()</code>.</p>

<h3>1. Key Questions & Analysis:</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Why use <code>password_hash()</code> instead of md5/sha1?</strong> MD5 and SHA-1 are fast and vulnerable to rainbow tables. <code>password_hash(..., PASSWORD_DEFAULT)</code> uses bcrypt, which includes automated salt generation and adjustable cryptographic cost factor.</li>
  <li><strong>How is duplicate check handled?</strong> <code>SELECT user_id FROM users WHERE email = ?</code> is executed prior to inserting records.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Key PHP Snippet (register.php):</h3>
<div class="code-box">$check = $pdo-&gt;prepare("SELECT user_id FROM users WHERE email = ?");
$check-&gt;execute([$email]);

if ($check-&gt;fetch()) {
  $message = "This email is already registered. Please login.";
} else {
  $hashedPassword = password_hash($password, PASSWORD_DEFAULT);
  $stmt = $pdo-&gt;prepare("INSERT INTO users (name, email, password, role) VALUES (?, ?, ?, 'student')");
  $stmt-&gt;execute([$name, $email, $hashedPassword]);
  
  // Also insert corresponding student profile
  $pdo-&gt;prepare("INSERT INTO students (name, email, mobile, department, semester) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)")
      -&gt;execute([$name, $email, $mobile, $course, $semester]);
}</div>

